TI  - A Critical Evaluation of Psilocybin-Assisted Therapy Protocol Components From Clinical Trial Patients, Facilitators, and Caregivers
T2  - PSYCHOTHERAPY
M3  - Article
AB  - Psilocybin-assisted therapy (PAT) is an experimental treatment with transformative promise. Developing standards for PAT psychotherapy protocols is a priority, but psychotherapeutic protocol components of PAT have been subjected to little rigorous research. This study was designed to assess protocol components in a trial of PAT. The Enhanced Critical Incident Technique (E-CIT) was used to identify critical incidents in the treatment, wish list items comprising components or modifications that would have improved the treatment experience, and contributing factors that influenced the treatment. Participants included patients (n = 10), facilitators (n = 7 licensed mental health clinicians and certified spiritual health clinicians), and caregivers (n = 7) in an open-label trial investigating PAT for cancer-related demoralization and chronic pain. Patients and caregivers were interviewed after their last treatment session in the trial; facilitators were interviewed at the end of the entire trial. Rapid qualitative analysis identified specific domains for improvement in the treatment protocol based on the E-CIT. Critical incidents, wish list items, and contributing factors pertained to aspects of the therapy (e.g., importance of intention-setting) and the overall protocol (e.g., navigating transitions in the treatment). Findings indicate the importance of tailoring PAT to accommodate the medical history and needs of this population, support common factors, and ensure collaborative care. Recommendations across nine topic areas were derived from the data and presented in the Discussion. The E-CIT shows promise for advancing early stage research on PAT components.Clinical Impact StatementQuestion: What psychotherapy components and characteristics should be retained or revised in psilocybin-assisted psychotherapy for cancer patients? Findings: Psychotherapy should be tailored to the medical needs of this population, emphasize common factors, employ multidisciplinary collaborative treatment teams, and strive toward flexibility to accommodate unique patient experiences. Meaning: Psychotherapy research in psilocybin-assisted therapy (PAT) is still nascent, but methods like the Enhanced Critical Incident Technique can reveal important information to improve this novel therapy. Next Steps: Studies examining other psychotherapy protocols in PAT, as well as evidencebased treatments incorporated with PAT, are needed.
