TY  - JOUR
AU  - Penzenstadler, Louise
AU  - Brakha, Tatiana Aboulafia
AU  - Zullino, Daniele
TI  - Depression and anxiety symptoms following psychedelic-assisted psychotherapy for treatment-resistant depression: retrospective routine data analysis with vs. without comorbid substance use disorder
T2  - SUCHTTHERAPIE
M3  - Article
AB  - Substance use disorders (SUD) are chronic conditions with high relapse rates and limited effectiveness of established treatments. Against this backdrop, psychedelic-assisted psychotherapy (PAP) with psilocybin and LSD has gained renewed interest; the literature reports signals suggesting potential effects, particularly in AUD and affective symptoms. Purpose To describe changes in depressive and anxiety symptoms after a single PAP session (psilocybin or LSD) in the naturalistic clinical setting of the Geneva PAP program and to explore whether comorbid SUD is associated with a different symptom trajectory. Methods Retrospective, uncontrolled evaluation of prospectively collected routine data from the PAP program at Geneva University Hospital. N=68 adults with treatment-resistant depression received one PAP session (psilocybin or LSD) in a standardized setting (preparation, session, integration). Depressive and anxiety symptoms (BDI and STAI-T) were assessed at screening, preparation (pre-dose) and 1 month. Outcomes: BDI (depression) and STAI-T (anxiety). The primary interval was preparation -> 1 month; screening was reported descriptively. Comparative analysis were performed for patients with comorbid SUD (n=17) versus without SUD (n=51). Results A significant time effect was observed for both BDI and STAI-T (p<0.001). There was no statistical evidence for a group effect or a group & times;time interaction (p>0.05). Given the uncontrolled observational design and sample size, non-significance should not be interpreted as evidence of equivalence. Conclusions In this regulated real-world setting, PAP was associated with short-term decreases in depressive and anxiety symptoms. We found no statistical evidence that comorbid SUD moderated symptom change; however, equivalence cannot be concluded. Controlled prospective studies with longer follow-up and SUD-specific outcomes are warranted
