TY  - JOUR
AU  - Prinz, Susanne
AU  - Aicher, Helena
TI  - Psychedelics in Psychotherapy: From Pharmacological Action to Transdiagnostic Change
T2  - SUCHTTHERAPIE
M3  - Article
AB  - Psychedelic-assisted therapy (PAT) has emerged in recent years as one of the most dynamic areas of psychiatric research. While the strongest evidence to date exists for depressive disorders and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), PAT is increasingly being explored in other non-psychotic disorders. Beyond symptom reduction, transdiagnostic processes such as enhanced acceptance, connectedness, and cognitive flexibility have come into focus. This review summarizes current findings on the therapeutic use of psychedelics, MDMA, and ketamine. It highlights neurobiological, psychological, and contextual mechanisms of action, presents existing clinical evidence, and discusses risks, contraindications, and conceptual challenges of clinical implementation. Current data indicate good tolerability under professional guidance and significant efficacy for depression and PTSD. Preliminary studies further suggest potential benefits for anxiety, obsessive-compulsive, and eating disorders, underscoring the transdiagnostic potential of PAT. PAT opens new perspectives for psychotherapy while at the same time challenging existing frameworks and inviting an expansion of therapeutic understanding beyond diagnostic categories.
